{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>When HaShem gives you in the evening meat to eat and bread in the morning to satisfy.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the reason the provision came at two times, one by day and one by night, and He did not give them everything in the morning — this is in accordance with the way of the world, that people eat two meals every day, one by day and one by night; as the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: ״תשבו כעין תדורו״ — 'you shall dwell as you reside' — just as residence is one by day and one by night, so too the sukkah, one by day and one by night." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> ״בערב בשר לאכול ולחם בבקר״ — from here you learn that the quail He gave them in dimness of face, but the manna, which they asked for according to the law, He gave them in the morning with shining faces, because there is time during the day for preparation." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> ״בערב בשר לאכול ולחם בבקר״ — He designated the provision at two times, this by day and this by night, corresponding to the attribute of judgment (middat ha-din) and the attribute of mercy (middat ha-rachamim). And this verse makes known that provision of meat comes about in the evening and provision of bread in the morning. And what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded in tractate Avodah Zarah — that at the third hour He sits and sustains the whole world, from the horns of wild oxen to the eggs of lice, in the provision of meat — it is speaking of that, for the hours of thirds of the day, out of the twelve hours that is the day, are called ערב; and thus it is written ״בין הערבים תאכלו בשר״ — the verse teaches you that there are two evenings: one evening is three thirds, the second evening is three quarters, when the sun is setting. And the meat would come to Israel between these two evenings; this is the meaning of ״בין הערבים תאכלו בשר״, and it is the time of slaughter of the Paschal offering, concerning which it is written (Leviticus 23): ״בין הערבים פסח לה׳״. But the provision of bread was in the morning, corresponding to the attribute of mercy; and this indicates the superiority of the manna, in which the intermediaries did not multiply." "<b>Your murmurings are not against us, but against HaShem.</b> We learn from here that whoever quarrels with a prophet or a Torah scholar is as if he quarrels with the Shekhinah, for Israel were murmuring against Moses and Aaron, as it is written ״וילונו על משה ועל אהרן במדבר״, and he says ״לא עלינו תלונותיכם כי על ה׳״; and likewise at the end of the parashah he says ״מה תריבון עמדי מה תנסון את ה׳״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:00:08.697888995Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}